---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { meta } from '../../lib/data.js';
const cov = meta.coverage;
---
<Base title="About — happenings"
      description="What happenings is, where its listings come from, and what they don't cover yet.">
  <p class="kicker" style="margin-top:26px">happenings</p>
  <h1 class="page">About</h1>
  <p class="lede">happenings is an index of what's on in New York, rebuilt every
    day from public event feeds. Every listing links back to whoever published
    it — this is somewhere to find things, not somewhere to buy the ticket.</p>

  <h2 class="sec">Where the listings come from</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13.5px">
    <tr>
      <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--ink);padding-bottom:6px">Source</th>
      <th style="text-align:left;border-bottom:2px solid var(--ink);padding-bottom:6px">Last checked</th>
    </tr>
    {meta.sources.map((s) => (
      <tr>
        <td style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--paper-3)">
          <b>{s.label}</b><br />
          <span style="color:var(--ink-2)">{s.attribution}</span>
        </td>
        <td style="padding:8px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--paper-3);color:var(--ink-2)">
          {new Date(s.fetched_at).toLocaleString('en-US', { dateStyle: 'medium', timeZone: meta.tz })}
        </td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </table>

  <h2 class="sec">What isn't here</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <ul style="max-width:70ch;line-height:1.65">
    <li><b>Prices.</b> None of these sources publish one, so nothing is marked
      free or paid. Check the source before you set out — many of the parks
      listings are free, but this site won't tell you that it knows.</li>
    <li><b>An exact spot for every event.</b> {cov.neighborhood}% are placed to a
      neighborhood. The rest give only a borough, so they'll show up in a
      borough filter but not on a block.</li>
    <li><b>Most ticketed venues.</b> A lot of the city's music, theatre and
      talks are published only on venues' own calendars, and those aren't all
      indexed yet.</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="max-width:70ch">Nothing here is estimated. Where a detail isn't
    published, it's left blank rather than guessed at.</p>

  <h2 class="sec">Corrections and takedowns</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <p style="max-width:70ch">Everything here is rebuilt from someone else's
    feed, which means a listing can be wrong in ways this site can't see. If
    you run a venue and something about you is wrong, out of date, or shouldn't
    be here at all — a listing, a photograph, the venue itself — write to
    <a href="mailto:hello@happenings.town">hello@happenings.town</a> and it
    will be fixed or removed. No form, no account, and you don't have to
    explain why.</p>
  <p style="max-width:70ch">Two things worth knowing before you write. Listings
    are rebuilt from the source every day, so a correction made at the source
    arrives here on its own within a day — and a correction made <i>here</i>
    alone would be overwritten by the next run. Photographs come from Wikimedia
    Commons and are credited to their authors below each one; they're linked
    from Commons rather than copied onto this site.</p>

  <h2 class="sec">Privacy</h2><div class="rule"></div>
  <p style="max-width:70ch">No accounts, no sign-in, and nothing here asks you
    for anything. Traffic is measured with Vercel Web Analytics, which sets no
    cookies and builds no profile across sites. Alongside the page view it
    records which listings get opened and which links leave the site — the
    event or place name, its neighbourhood and category, and the page it
    happened on. That's a measure of whether the index is doing its one job,
    which is sending you somewhere else. It isn't tied to you, and there is
    nothing to opt out of because there is nothing identifying you to begin
    with. Questions to the same address.</p>
</Base>
